.md
Formatting in WLJS is not limited to text. Numbers, formulas, lists, tables,
graphics, controls, and even complete interfaces are all Wolfram Language
expressions, and the same formatting functions can be composed around them.

If you are coming from Python or Julia, it helps to read the following examples
with three small syntax rules in mind:

- function arguments use square brackets: `Style[expr, Red]`
- lists use braces: `{a, b, c}`
- options are rules: `Background -> Yellow`
- `name = value` assigns a value, and a final semicolon suppresses its output

Wolfram Language also supports postfix notation. These two expressions are
equivalent:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Formatting in WLJS is not limited to text. Numbers, formulas, lists, tables,
graphics, controls, and even complete interfaces are all Wolfram Language
expressions, and the same formatting functions can be composed around them.

If you are coming from Python or Julia, it helps to read the following examples
with three small syntax rules in mind:

- function arguments use square brackets: `Style[expr, Red]`
- lists use braces: `{a, b, c}`
- options are rules: `Background -> Yellow`
- `name = value` assigns a value, and a final semicolon suppresses its output

Wolfram Language also supports postfix notation. These two expressions are
equivalent:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
TraditionalForm[expr]  (* equivalent to expr // TraditionalForm *)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Prefix notation is common when several wrappers are composed:
`function @ expr` is equivalent to `function[expr]`.

We shall start with common display tasks, then explains the WLJS-specific
behavior that makes formatted output editable and reusable.

## Choose the right tool!

| Description | Expression |
| --- | --- |
| Control decimal places or significant digits | [`NumberForm`](./../Formatting/NumberForm) |
| Inspect a copyable, one-line raw representation | [`InputForm`](./../Formatting/InputForm) |
| Display conventional mathematical notation | [`TraditionalForm`](./../Formatting/TraditionalForm) |
| Arrange expressions horizontally or vertically | [`Row`](./../Formatting/Row), [`Column`](./../Formatting/Column) |
| Build a table or matrix | [`Grid`](./../Formatting/Grid), [`TableForm`](./../Formatting/TableForm), [`MatrixForm`](./../Formatting/MatrixForm) |
| Change color, font, size, or background | [`Style`](./../Formatting/Style) |
| Add a frame, panel, label, or fixed-size area | [`Framed`](./../Formatting/Framed), [`Panel`](./../Formatting/Panel), [`Labeled`](./../Formatting/Labeled), [`Pane`](./../Formatting/Pane) |
| Make a finished result non-editable | [`Deploy`](./../Formatting/Deploy) |
| Collapse a large expression | [`Iconize`](./../Formatting/Iconize) |
| Export or display LaTeX | [`TeXForm`](./../Formatting/TeXForm), [`TeXView`](./../GUI/TeXView) |

<Callout type="info">
Most examples in this guide change how an expression is displayed, not the data
you should use for computation. A reliable pattern is to keep raw data in one
symbol and apply formatting only when producing output.
</Callout>

## Formatting numbers

Wolfram Language distinguishes exact and approximate numbers. For example,
`1/3` is exact, while `N[1/3]` produces a machine-precision approximation.
[`NumberForm`](./../Formatting/NumberForm) controls how a number is shown:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Prefix notation is common when several wrappers are composed:
`function @ expr` is equivalent to `function[expr]`.

We shall start with common display tasks, then explains the WLJS-specific
behavior that makes formatted output editable and reusable.

## Choose the right tool!

| Description | Expression |
| --- | --- |
| Control decimal places or significant digits | [`NumberForm`](./../Formatting/NumberForm) |
| Inspect a copyable, one-line raw representation | [`InputForm`](./../Formatting/InputForm) |
| Display conventional mathematical notation | [`TraditionalForm`](./../Formatting/TraditionalForm) |
| Arrange expressions horizontally or vertically | [`Row`](./../Formatting/Row), [`Column`](./../Formatting/Column) |
| Build a table or matrix | [`Grid`](./../Formatting/Grid), [`TableForm`](./../Formatting/TableForm), [`MatrixForm`](./../Formatting/MatrixForm) |
| Change color, font, size, or background | [`Style`](./../Formatting/Style) |
| Add a frame, panel, label, or fixed-size area | [`Framed`](./../Formatting/Framed), [`Panel`](./../Formatting/Panel), [`Labeled`](./../Formatting/Labeled), [`Pane`](./../Formatting/Pane) |
| Make a finished result non-editable | [`Deploy`](./../Formatting/Deploy) |
| Collapse a large expression | [`Iconize`](./../Formatting/Iconize) |
| Export or display LaTeX | [`TeXForm`](./../Formatting/TeXForm), [`TeXView`](./../GUI/TeXView) |

<Callout type="info">
Most examples in this guide change how an expression is displayed, not the data
you should use for computation. A reliable pattern is to keep raw data in one
symbol and apply formatting only when producing output.
</Callout>

## Formatting numbers

Wolfram Language distinguishes exact and approximate numbers. For example,
`1/3` is exact, while `N[1/3]` produces a machine-precision approximation.
[`NumberForm`](./../Formatting/NumberForm) controls how a number is shown:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
value = N[Pi];
NumberForm[value, {5, 3}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(3.141592653589793)(*,*)(*"1:eJxdT11LwzAALNOB+C/cU5uJMF19GPiQlEWY1Em+ah0+dFkmoenSruva+RP81Uv36Mtxdxzc3d3aku2V53n1rYP5Rh/sXmjV0j8nfYDQCgT+08Nk+hj44N5xMJrM1KJjLLYftHjP01dow5+YtjQRyyxvKrkLSZqbMRcli1CHNSRiUbFEtxEvI/LZhZKX9MuMn2UuNC/wiSaGyF9YcTk/xm98iiFMCyzsy6jv+0YIBNtBP/HaAWmMojc9UdlmuTOni8v2jfqX6f9QZZQ8ZGuj6qGTODO1OgMpmUK8"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
You can still copy it and use as input too. The expression will expand back to it's original form:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
You can still copy it and use as input too. The expression will expand back to it's original form:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(3.141592653589793)(*,*)(*"1:eJxdT11LwzAALNOB+C/cU5uJMF19GPiQlEWY1Em+ah0+dFkmoenSruva+RP81Uv36Mtxdxzc3d3aku2V53n1rYP5Rh/sXmjV0j8nfYDQCgT+08Nk+hj44N5xMJrM1KJjLLYftHjP01dow5+YtjQRyyxvKrkLSZqbMRcli1CHNSRiUbFEtxEvI/LZhZKX9MuMn2UuNC/wiSaGyF9YcTk/xm98iiFMCyzsy6jv+0YIBNtBP/HaAWmMojc9UdlmuTOni8v2jfqX6f9QZZQ8ZGuj6qGTODO1OgMpmUK8"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
3.141592653589793`

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
In `{5, 3}`, the first value is the number of significant digits to print and
the second is the number of digits to the right of the decimal point.

You can format every number in a larger expression at once:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
In `{5, 3}`, the first value is the number of significant digits to print and
the second is the number of digits to the right of the decimal point.

You can format every number in a larger expression at once:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
values = N[{Pi, Sqrt[2], 1/7}];
NumberForm[values, {5, 3}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
{(*VB[*)(3.141592653589793)(*,*)(*"1:eJxdT11LwzAALNOB+C/cU5uJMF19GPiQlEWY1Em+ah0+dFkmoenSruva+RP81Uv36Mtxdxzc3d3aku2V53n1rYP5Rh/sXmjV0j8nfYDQCgT+08Nk+hj44N5xMJrM1KJjLLYftHjP01dow5+YtjQRyyxvKrkLSZqbMRcli1CHNSRiUbFEtxEvI/LZhZKX9MuMn2UuNC/wiSaGyF9YcTk/xm98iiFMCyzsy6jv+0YIBNtBP/HaAWmMojc9UdlmuTOni8v2jfqX6f9QZZQ8ZGuj6qGTODO1OgMpmUK8"*)(*]VB*),(*VB[*)(1.4142135623730951)(*,*)(*"1:eJxdT11LwzAALOpA/Bfuqc1EKLQ+CHtIyiJM6iRftcoeuiyT0HRp13Xt9hP81aY++nLcHQd3d7+xZHfteV5752Cx1Ud7EFr19MdJHyD0BQI/fIzCKPDBg+NgGj6r5cBYat9p9VbmL9DG3yntaSZWRdk1ch+TvDQzLmqWoAFrSMSyYZnuE14n5GOIJa/pp5k9yVJoXuEzzQyRF9hwuTilrzzCEOYVFnY+HfvWCIFgdzVOvHFAOqPo7UhUsV3tzfnPZYdO/cuMf6gySh6LjVHtxElcmFb9AimUQrw="*)(*]VB*),(*VB[*)(0.14285714285714285)(*,*)(*"1:eJxdT8tqwkAADH1A6V/UU7KWotT0IPSwG9yCJVr2laalh7iuZcnGTYxpop/gV7vx2MswMwzMzMPKks2153n1vYPZWu/tTmjV0pOTPkDoGwT+6Gk8eQ588Og4GIynat4xFtsPWizy9A3a8DemLU3EMsubSm5DkuZmyEXJItRhDYmYVyzRbcTLiHx2oeQl/TLDF5kLzQt8oIkh8ggrLmd/8TufYAjTAgv7Ouj7fhACweaqn3jjgDRG0bueqGy93JrDxWW7Rv3L9H+oMkrus5VR9a2TODO1OgMn+0K6"*)(*]VB*)}

