.md
Apply `Deploy` last so the output remains easy to inspect and edit while you are
building it.

## Labels and legends in plots

Plot labels do not have to be strings. You can pass formatted expressions
directly:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Apply `Deploy` last so the output remains easy to inspect and edit while you are
building it.

## Labels and legends in plots

Plot labels do not have to be strings. You can pass formatted expressions
directly:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Plot[
  x^2,
  {x, 0, 1},
  Frame -> True,
  FrameLabel -> {
    "x",
    Row[{TraditionalForm[x^2], " value"}]
  }
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["9fea110f-2aa2-4f5b-bc95-c1bf1bf4666f"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKW6alJhoaGqTpGiUmGumapJkm6SYlW5rqJhsmpQGRiZmZWRoAklUWWw=="*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
[`Placed`](./../Formatting/Placed) controls the position of legends and other
supported elements. [`TeXView`](./../GUI/TeXView) can render a label supplied as
LaTeX:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
[`Placed`](./../Formatting/Placed) controls the position of legends and other
supported elements. [`TeXView`](./../GUI/TeXView) can render a label supplied as
LaTeX:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Plot[
  x^2,
  {x, 0, 1},
  PlotLegends -> Placed[
    {TeXView["x^2"]},
    {0.2, 0.7}
  ]
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(Legended[ToExpression[FrontEndRef["c30ce5a4-067a-43ef-a6e1-490668e888dc"], InputForm], Placed[LineLegend[{Directive[Opacity[1.], RGBColor[0.24, 0.6, 0.8], AbsoluteThickness[2]]}, {TeXView[Offload[URLDecode["x%5E2"]]]}, LegendMarkers -> None, LabelStyle -> {}, LegendLayout -> "Column"], {0.2, 0.7}, Identity]])(*,*)(*"1:"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Use `Row` when a label combines mathematical and ordinary content:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Use `Row` when a label combines mathematical and ordinary content:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Plot[
  x^2,
  {x, 0, 1},
  PlotLegends -> Placed[
    {Row[{TeXView["x^2"], Spacer[5], "function"}]},
    {0.2, 0.7}
  ]
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(Legended[ToExpression[FrontEndRef["29c2351b-cd23-4b94-8826-256492254696"], InputForm], Placed[LineLegend[{Directive[Opacity[1.], RGBColor[0.24, 0.6, 0.8], AbsoluteThickness[2]]}, {Row[{TeXView[Offload[URLDecode["x%5E2"]]], Spacer[5], "function"}]}, LegendMarkers -> None, LabelStyle -> {}, LegendLayout -> "Column"], {0.2, 0.7}, Identity]])(*,*)(*"1:"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Place expressions inside graphics

[`Inset`](./../Graphics/Inset) places an arbitrary expression on a
two-dimensional graphics canvas. This can be text, held Wolfram Language,
another graphic, an image, or a UI view.

Use `HoldForm` when the displayed expression should not evaluate:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Place expressions inside graphics

[`Inset`](./../Graphics/Inset) places an arbitrary expression on a
two-dimensional graphics canvas. This can be text, held Wolfram Language,
another graphic, an image, or a UI view.

Use `HoldForm` when the displayed expression should not evaluate:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Plot[
  x,
  {x, 0, 10},
  Epilog -> {
    Inset[HoldForm[1 + 1], {5, 5}, Center]
  }
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["87c8f413-5a8b-4fbb-bb12-1e20955d8a61"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKW5gnW6SZGBrrmiZaJOmapCUl6SYlGRrpGqYaGViamqZYJJoZAgCGbhWf"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Another complete graphic can be inset as well:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Another complete graphic can be inset as well:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
smallPlot = Plot[
  Sin[x],
  {x, 0, 10},
  Frame -> True,
  FrameTicks -> None,
  ImageSize -> 120
];

Graphics[{
  Circle[{0, 0}, 2],
  Inset[smallPlot, {0, 0}, Center, {2.5, 1.5}]
}, PlotRange -> 3, ImageSize -> 300]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["0df6e31f-793d-4d6e-9d78-5dc44118696d"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKG6SkmaUaG6bpmlsap+iapJil6lqmmFvomqYkm5gYGlqYWZqlAACIXBWP"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## LaTeX conversion

[`TeXForm`](./../Formatting/TeXForm) exports a Wolfram expression as a LaTeX
string:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## LaTeX conversion

[`TeXForm`](./../Formatting/TeXForm) exports a Wolfram expression as a LaTeX
string:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
tex = TeXForm[Series[Sin[x], {x, 0, 7}] // Normal]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
"-\\frac{x^7}{5040}+\\frac{x^5}{120}-\\frac{x^3}{6}+x"

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
`Normal` converts the truncated series object to an ordinary polynomial before
it is exported.

That string can be rendered with [`TeXView`](./../GUI/TeXView):

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
`Normal` converts the truncated series object to an ordinary polynomial before
it is exported.

That string can be rendered with [`TeXView`](./../GUI/TeXView):

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
TeXView[tex]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(Null)(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRAeO5AISY0Iy0wtRwj4p6Xl5CdCVXACidAgH5fU5PyU1OBIIE9X1dQ5rSgxWdXcqULV1NVc1dwFyDQ1MDEAsYycUKVNIdKGRiBZNK3GEDkziL4KAIJqJTI="*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
It can also be pasted into a [Markdown](./../Cell-types/Markdown) cell:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
It can also be pasted into a [Markdown](./../Cell-types/Markdown) cell:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
.md

$$
-\frac{x^7}{5040}+\frac{x^5}{120}-\frac{x^3}{6}+x
$$

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Or placed directly in a LaTeX cell:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Or placed directly in a LaTeX cell:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.latex

-\frac{x^7}{5040}+\frac{x^5}{120}-\frac{x^3}{6}+x

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
For supported LaTeX input, `ToExpression` performs the reverse conversion:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
For supported LaTeX input, `ToExpression` performs the reverse conversion:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ToExpression["\\frac{x^5}{120}-\\frac{x^3}{6}+x", TeXForm]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Do not confuse `TeXForm` with `TraditionalForm`: `TraditionalForm` is an
in-notebook visual representation, while `TeXForm` produces text for
interchange with LaTeX-based tools.

<Callout type="note">
`TeXForm` is blocking. For buttons, animations, timers, and other interactive
code, use the non-blocking [`TeXFormAsync`](./../Formatting/TeXFormAsync)
variant.
</Callout>

## Compact large expressions

Large lists, graphics options, and generated expressions can make a notebook
difficult to navigate. [`Iconize`](./../Formatting/Iconize) replaces an
expression with a compact icon:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Do not confuse `TeXForm` with `TraditionalForm`: `TraditionalForm` is an
in-notebook visual representation, while `TeXForm` produces text for
interchange with LaTeX-based tools.

<Callout type="note">
`TeXForm` is blocking. For buttons, animations, timers, and other interactive
code, use the non-blocking [`TeXFormAsync`](./../Formatting/TeXFormAsync)
variant.
</Callout>

## Compact large expressions

Large lists, graphics options, and generated expressions can make a notebook
difficult to navigate. [`Iconize`](./../Formatting/Iconize) replaces an
expression with a compact icon:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
data = Range[100] // Iconize

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(Uncompress["1:eJwl0jtOQzEQhtHLYyPsIJnxY7yEK6ViCQghUVGE/Yvg0/yfmzmV3z5+3r8+j+O4vz7m9n3/PZ8ej+ve2Jt7296+d+yde2vvOp//ry5ylZCUJl2GTCmhBCUoQQlKUIISlKAEJShJSUpSkpKUpCQlKUlJSqM0SqM0SqM0SqM0SqM0Sqd0Sqd0Sqd0Sqd0Sqd0yqAMyqAMyqAMyqAMyqAMyqRMyqRMyqRMyqRMyqRMSlGKUpSiFKUoRSlKUYqyKIuyKIuyKIuyKIuyKGudL/vXXf4AYh9I6w=="])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNmYGAo5gUSYZmp5S6pyflFiSX5RcEcQBHP5Py8zKrUlEwHRgaGNCaQQhYgEVSakxrMCmT4JCal5gSDhPLy87MB5DYSYw=="*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
You can also select an expression—or a sequence of expressions—and choose
**Iconize** from the context menu or the
[command palette](./Command-palette).

Small iconized values are compressed into the notebook text. Expressions larger
than 30,000 bytes are stored in the `.iconized/` directory next to the notebook
and referenced by the icon.

<Callout type="warning">
If a notebook contains file-backed icons, keep its `.iconized/` directory when
moving, sharing, or versioning the notebook.
</Callout>

Iconization changes how much source is visible, not what the expression
represents. It is useful for embedded constants and generated arguments, but
named variables or external data files are usually clearer when the same data
is reused in several places.

## When to use HTML or WLX instead

`Style`, `Grid`, and related functions are a good fit for symbolic output that
should remain close to Wolfram Language input. For a complete application page,
responsive web layout, custom CSS, or reusable interface components, use
[HTML cells](./../Cell-types/HTML) or [WLX cells](./../Cell-types/WLX).

See [Styling with HTML](./Styling-HTML) for a guided introduction. Wolfram
expressions can be embedded in those cells, and `StandardForm` can be used when
you want their ordinary WLJS output-cell appearance.

For custom formatting of your own symbolic heads, continue with
[`MakeBoxes`](./../Formatting/MakeBoxes) and the
[Decorating symbols](./../Advanced/Decorating-symbols) guide.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
